To create an interactive check-in page where the youth can click on their emotions and see a corresponding scripture in a popup, we'll need to make a few improvements to your existing code. Below, I've outlined how to fix the issues and enhance the functionality you're looking for.

### HTML Structure

First, we'll make sure your HTML is structured correctly. You should have separate elements for each emotion so that each can trigger its respective popup.

```html
<div class="emotion-container">
    <div class="happy-thumb popup" onclick="showPopup('happyPopup')">
        <p>Happy</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sad-thumb popup" onclick="showPopup('sadPopup')">
        <p>Sad</p>
    </div>
    <div class="angry-thumb popup" onclick="showPopup('angryPopup')">
        <p>Angry</p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Hidden popups -->
<div class="popup-content" id="happyPopup">I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Wonderful are your works; my soul knows it very well. Psalm 139:14</div>
<div class="popup-content" id="sadPopup">The Lord is near to the brokenhearted and saves the crushed in spirit. Psalm 34:18</div>
<div class="popup-content" id="angryPopup">Be angry, and do not sin; do not let the sun go down on your anger. Ephesians 4:26</div>

<div class="overlay" id="popupOverlay" onclick="hidePopup()"></div>

<!-- Styles -->
<style>
    .popup-content {
        display: none; /* Hidden by default */
        position: fixed;
        background-color: white;
        border: 1px solid #ccc;
        padding: 10px;
        z-index: 10;
        width: 300px;
        left: 50%;
        top: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    }
    .overlay {
        display: none; /* Hidden by default */
        position: fixed;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
        z-index: 5;
    }
    .show {
        display: block; /* Show the popup */
    }
</style>
```

### JavaScript Functionality

The JavaScript function must be responsible for showing and hiding the popups. Here’s the modified version:

```javascript
<script>
    function showPopup(popupId) {
        var popup = document.getElementById(popupId);
        var overlay = document.getElementById("popupOverlay");

        // Show the popup and overlay
        popup.classList.add("show");
        overlay.style.display = "block";
    }

    function hidePopup() {
        var popups = document.getElementsByClassName("popup-content");
        var overlay = document.getElementById("popupOverlay");

        // Hide all popups
        Array.from(popups).forEach(function(popup) {
            popup.classList.remove("show");
        });

        // Hide the overlay
        overlay.style.display = "none";
    }
</script>
```

### Explanation of Changes

1. **HTML Structure**: Each emotion is a separate div. The popups are now hidden content divs corresponding to each emotion but are not included within the clickable areas. This maintains clarity.

2. **Popup Management**: I've added a more general `showPopup` function that accepts the ID of the popup related to the emotion clicked. This makes it flexible and easy to extend with additional emotions.

3. **Overlay**: An overlay is introduced that darkens the rest of the page when a popup is shown, enhancing the user experience. Clicking on the overlay also closes the popup.

4. **CSS for Popup**: The styles ensure that the popups are centered and visually distinct.

### Final Notes

Make sure to test the functionality of your popups across different browsers and devices to ensure compatibility. This setup allows for easy addition of more emotions and associated scriptures, all while maintaining a clean and user-friendly interface.

