To allow null date values in Spring MVC while using a custom date formatter, you'll need to configure your `DateFormatter` to handle blank inputs as valid values. Here is how you can achieve that:

### Step 1: Custom Date Formatter

You can extend the `DateFormatter` or create a custom formatter that checks if the input string is empty or whitespace before attempting to parse it. If it is, the formatter can return `null`.

Here’s an example of a custom formatter:

```java
import org.springframework.format.datetime.DateFormatter;

import java.text.ParseException;
import java.util.Date;

public class NullableDateFormatter extends DateFormatter {

    public NullableDateFormatter(String pattern) {
        super(pattern);
    }

    @Override
    public Date parse(String text, java.util.Locale locale) throws ParseException {
        if (text == null || text.trim().isEmpty()) {
            return null; // Allow null for empty strings
        }
        return super.parse(text, locale);
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Register the Custom Formatter

You will need to register this custom formatter with the Spring application context in your configuration. You can do this in your XML configuration or Java-based configuration. Below is a simple XML configuration for registering the formatter:

```xml
<beans:bean id="conversionService"
    class="org.springframework.format.support.FormattingConversionServiceFactoryBean">
    <beans:property name="formatters">
        <beans:set>
            <beans:bean class="com.example.NullableDateFormatter">
                <beans:constructor-arg>
                    <beans:value>dd/MM/yyyy</beans:value>
                </beans:constructor-arg>
            </beans:bean>
        </beans:set>
    </beans:property>
</beans:bean>
```

### Step 3: Configure @InitBinder (if needed)

If your controller uses `@InitBinder`, you can still register the custom editor there, but note that this is more common for specific cases when you're dealing with other types of data.

For instance:

```java
@Controller
public class MyController {

    @InitBinder
    public void initBinder(WebDataBinder binder) {
        binder.registerCustomEditor(Date.class, new NullableDateFormatter("dd/MM/yyyy"));
    }

    // your controller methods
}
```

### Step 4: Test with Controller Method

You can then test this parser with a controller method that expects a nullable date:

```java
@PostMapping("/submit")
public String submitForm(@RequestParam(required = false) Date myDate) {
    // myDate will be null if the input is empty or whitespace
    return "result";
}
```

### In Summary

The key to allowing null date values is to create a custom date formatter that checks for empty strings and appropriately returns `null` instead of throwing an exception. Register this new formatter in your conversion service, and it should work with fields in your forms that are left blank.

This will allow you to handle date inputs in a way that respects both the format you're interested in and the possibility of receiving no input at all.

