Q: error in online c compiler when using typedef __unaligned struct __attribute__((packed))
A: <p>Neither <code>__unaligned</code> nor <code>__attribute__((packed))</code> is standard C. In fact there is no standard way to disable struct packing.</p>
<p>One way to make it portable is to hide all non-standard extensions behind preprocessor macros:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>    #ifdef __arm__  // &quot;Keil&quot; compiler, nowadays the &quot;ARM&quot; compiler
      typedef __unaligned struct __attribute__((packed))
    #elif defined(__GNUC__)
      typedef struct __attribute__((packed)) __attribute__((aligned(1)))

    /* and so on, add support for whatever compilers you need here */
    #endif
    {

        int a;
        int b;
    }a_b;
</code></pre>
<p>Another option is to use <code>#pragma</code>, if supported by the compiler. <code>#pragma PACK(1)</code> is a common non-standard extension which does the same thing as <code>__attribute__((packed))</code>. It is at least supported by gcc but I don't know about Keil.</p>
<p>The advantage of pragmas is that a compiler which doesn't recognize a certain pragma just skips it - they were meant to be used for the very purpose of hiding non-portable code.</p>

