To achieve a 3-way data binding with UI-Grid and Firebase in your AngularJS application, you need to ensure that when changes are made in the UI-Grid, they are reflected back to Firebase and properly synced with the UI-Grid data model. Let's go over the modifications needed to your existing setup to effectively implement this behavior.

### Updated HTML View
Your HTML view for the UI-Grid remains essentially the same, but ensure you have the following directive for row editing.

```html
<!-- UI-Grid -->
<div ui-grid="gridOptions" class="ui-grid" ui-grid-resize-columns ui-grid-edit ui-grid-row-edit ui-grid-cellNav></div>
```

### Updated Controller Code
You'll need to set up your grid options, especially focusing on the `onRegisterApi` function to incorporate saving changes to Firebase on cell edits. Here's a more complete implementation:

```javascript
var ref = new Firebase("https://commentme.firebaseio.com/comments");    

// Create an AngularFire reference to the data
var sync = $firebase(ref);

// Create a synchronized array for use in our HTML code
$scope.comments = sync.$asArray();

$scope.newComment = { text: '', date: '' };

// Push data back to Firebase Array
$scope.addComment = function() {
    $scope.comments.$add($scope.newComment);           
    $scope.newComment = { text: '', date: '' };
};

// UI Grid Options  
$scope.gridOptions = {
    enableSorting: true,
    columnDefs: [
        { name: 'comments', field: 'text', cellTemplate: '<div ng-class="{ editing: row.entity.editing }">{{ COL_FIELD }}</div>' },
        { name: 'date', field: 'date' }
    ],
    data: $scope.comments,
    onRegisterApi: function(gridApi){
        // Set the gridApi on the scope for later use
        $scope.gridApi = gridApi;

        // Listen for row edit save
        gridApi.rowEdit.on.saveRow($scope, $scope.saveRow);
    }
};

$scope.saveRow = function(rowEntity) { 
    // Save the changes to Firebase and return the promise
    $scope.comments.$save(rowEntity).then(function(ref) {
        console.log("Saved to Firebase:", ref);
    }).catch(function(error) {
        console.error("Error saving to Firebase:", error);
    });

    // Create a fake promise to use with UI-Grid
    var promise = $q.defer();
    $scope.gridApi.rowEdit.setSavePromise($scope.gridApi.grid, rowEntity, promise.promise);

    // Resolve the promise after a short delay (simulating an async operation)
    $interval(function() {
        if (rowEntity.text === '' || rowEntity.date === '') {
            promise.reject();
        } else {
            promise.resolve();
        }
    }, 1000, 1);
};
```

### Explanation
1. **Grid Registration**: Use the `onRegisterApi` function to assign `gridApi` to the `$scope`. This gives you access to UI-Grid's API for managing rows and their edits.

2. **Row Save Functionality**: The `saveRow` function is triggered when you attempt to edit a row in the UI-Grid:
   - It saves the edited row back to Firebase through the synchronized `$scope.comments` array.
   - The `then` callback can be used to handle post-save functionality.
   - The promise is constructed to inform UI-Grid when the save operation has completed. This allows users to edit the cell again only after the save resolves.

3. **Error Handling**: Make sure to handle any errors that arise from the `$save` method to ensure your application remains robust.

4. **Input Validations**: If your records have validations (like fields should not be empty), you check those conditions before resolving the promise.

### Note
If you're using AngularFire 2 or later, you would no longer directly interact with the Firebase reference but use AngularFire's higher-level services such as AngularFireDatabase for a more seamless integration.

This updated approach will enable the 3-way data binding effectively with UI-Grid and Firebase, maintaining the sync between front-end changes and your Firebase database.

