Q: $compile stripping inner contents of manipulated tags in IE
A: <p>As mentioned above, a solution was found thanks to a helpful suggestion by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/854556/amy-blankenship">@Amy Blackenship</a>.</p>

<p><strong>Original Problem Code</strong></p>

<pre><code>var injection = $(element).find ('&gt; div');
$(injection).attr ('ng-if', 'true');
$(element).html ('');
$(element).append ($compile (injection) ($scope));
</code></pre>

<p><strong>Updated Code</strong></p>

<pre><code>var injection = $(element).find ('&gt; div');
$(injection).attr ('ng-if', 'true');
$compile (injection) ($scope);
</code></pre>

<p><strong>So what changed?</strong></p>

<p>My newfound understanding of how $compile works is that the element gets angularized inline, not locally. What this means is that running $compile in the given scope does not clone and return the result of the operation, but instead changes the referenced element as it existed prior to the operation. In my example above, I'm selecting an element that already exists on the page, so when I clear the HTML it gets destroyed in the DOM while the reference to it still exists in my code, and when it's re-injected there's a conflict that IE doesn't really know how to rectify. Running $compile on the element directly without the DOM manipulation step bypasses this conflict.</p>

<p>In the case of examples 1 and 2 in the pen, the element that's getting manipulated was never on the page to begin with, so the reference is already local and as such no conflict exists when it gets injected into the page. In fact, I could inject it before the compilation step and it would still work.</p>

"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/QsdutXan.png" alt="Image" /></a></p>
<p>It is also suggested you add <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/process/resources?view=azure-devops#containers-resource-definition" rel="nofollow noreferrer">container resources in a YAML pipeline</a>, which supports continuous resource trigger upon pushing new tag of image to ACR even if it references the ARM service connection using WIF.</p>
<pre class="lang-yaml prettyprint-override"><code>resources:
  containers:
  - container: MyACR
    type: acr
    azureSubscription: ARMSvcCnnWIFSub0
    resourceGroup: rg-azacr
    registry: azacrxxxxxx
    repository: containerapp/dotnet/samplerepo1
    trigger: true

pool:
  vmImage: ubuntu-latest

steps:
- script: |
    echo &quot;MyACR.type - $(resources.container.MyACR.type)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.registry - $(resources.container.MyACR.registry)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.repository - $(resources.container.MyACR.repository)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.tag - $(resources.container.MyACR.tag)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.digest - $(resources.container.MyACR.digest)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.URI - $(resources.container.MyACR.URI)&quot;
    echo &quot;MyACR.location - $(resources.container.MyACR.location)&quot;
</code></pre>

