To implement functionality that closes your toggle menu when clicking outside of it, you can add an event listener to the document that checks if a click event occurred outside of your navigation menu and burger icon. If the click is outside both elements, you can close the menu.

Here’s a step-by-step implementation based on your provided HTML and JavaScript:

1. **Add an event listener to the document that listens for clicks.**
2. **Check if the clicked target is not the burger or any of the links in the navigation.**
3. **If so, toggle the "open" class on the navigation menu to close it.**

Below is the updated JavaScript code with the added functionality:

### JavaScript Code
```javascript
const burger = document.querySelector(".burger");
const navLinks = document.querySelector(".header-nav-links");
const links = document.querySelectorAll(".header-nav-links li");

//Toggle Nav
burger.addEventListener("click", () => {
    navLinks.classList.toggle("open");

    //Animate Links
    links.forEach((link, index) => {
        if (link.style.animation) {
            link.style.animation = ""
        } else {
            link.style.animation = `navLinkFade 0.5s ease forwards ${index / 7 + 0.2}s`;
        }
    });
});

// Close menu when clicking outside of it
document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    const isClickInside = navLinks.contains(event.target) || burger.contains(event.target);

    if (!isClickInside && navLinks.classList.contains("open")) {
        navLinks.classList.remove("open");
        links.forEach(link => link.style.animation = ""); // Reset animations
    }
});
```

### Explanation:
- **`const isClickInside = navLinks.contains(event.target) || burger.contains(event.target);`**: This line checks if the click occurred inside the navigation links or on the burger icon. The `.contains()` method checks if the specified node is a descendant of the calling node.
- **`if (!isClickInside && navLinks.classList.contains("open")) { ... }`**: This condition verifies if the click is outside both elements and if the navigation menu is currently open. If both conditions are met, we proceed to close the menu.
- **`navLinks.classList.remove("open");`**: This line removes the "open" class from the navigation menu to hide it.
- **Resetting animations**: When closing the menu, it's also a good idea to reset the animations to ensure they replay correctly the next time the menu is opened. 

### Conclusion
With these changes, clicking anywhere outside of the toggle burger icon or the open navigation menu will close the menu. This improves user experience by allowing for easy dismissal of the navigation drawer.

